#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083324 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179e0482-8d67-5e28-af2d-0d3aa7f0b770" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Given a Normal Distribution Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a8235a-b96e-5fa1-8108-e0e6dd4ab2d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516146} {:answer/id #uuid "4dccfb7c-69d9-5e39-bdc6-b9c1f9d8666a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516272} {:answer/id #uuid "4e878814-1cec-558c-9ad5-c9d1d1636938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516273} {:answer/id #uuid "5923c2ed-ea1c-5b9b-918c-6ab0788b0527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516302} {:answer/id #uuid "70ba8ee7-e027-558d-ba3b-6caf25bcdd99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e878814-1cec-558c-9ad5-c9d1d1636938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516273} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a80460b-81a6-5762-8a66-e56c03dc6782" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a1059d5-573e-59f8-8d92-01104e325d82" :question/math-academy-id "q-132444" :question/problem "A player's height in a particular professional basketball league is normally distributed with a mean of $77$ inches and a standard deviation of $3.57$ inches.\nWhat is the probability that the height of a randomly selected player is more than $76$ inches but less than $78$ inches?\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.1\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5636\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5675\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5714\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6026\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6064\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6103\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.3\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6406\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6443\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6480\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3594\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3557\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3520\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3974\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3936\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3897\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4364\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4325\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4286\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the height (in inches) of a randomly selected player. Then we have $X∼N(77,{3.57}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(76<X<78).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(76<X<78) & =P(\\frac{76-77}{3.57}<Z<\\frac{78-77}{3.57}) \\\\  & =P(-0.28<Z<0.28)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.28<Z<0.28) & =P(Z<0.28)-P(Z\\le -0.28) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.28)-P(Z\\le -0.28) \\\\  & =Φ(0.28)-Φ(-0.28)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.28) & =0.6103 \\\\ Φ(-0.28) & =0.3897.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.28<Z<0.28) & =Φ(0.28)-Φ(-0.28) \\\\  & =0.6103-0.3897 \\\\  & =0.2206 \\\\  & ≈0.22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the height of a randomly selected player is more than $76$ inches but less than $78$ inches is approximately $0.22.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083324 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179e0482-8d67-5e28-af2d-0d3aa7f0b770" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Given a Normal Distribution Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e35f18e-1bf6-5d1a-8e59-13ab06067872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516355} {:answer/id #uuid "9f019a42-6b8b-554e-91e3-893c44124a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516437} {:answer/id #uuid "cccb4d2d-b8aa-572e-aae2-f7163ea703a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516544} {:answer/id #uuid "e80d0d28-5add-54e4-97f4-c1b73ae40982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516593} {:answer/id #uuid "fb074b86-3190-5f6d-8a8e-9fbdb9461d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f019a42-6b8b-554e-91e3-893c44124a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516437} :answer-field/id #uuid "606b941c-af91-539a-a82d-135b0666034b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb189cdf-2b97-5db2-ae7a-d4991a5c036b" :question/math-academy-id "q-132711" :question/problem "The annual income of an employee in a large company is normally distributed with a mean of $$50\\,000$ and a standard deviation of $$15\\,000.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected employee's income is more than $$46\\,700$ but less than $$56\\,150?$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4207\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4168\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4129\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4602\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4562\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4522\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5000\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4960\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4920\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5793\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5832\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5871\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.3\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6179\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6217\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6255\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.4\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6554\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6591\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6628\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the annual income (in dollars) of a randomly selected employee. Then we have $X∼N(50\\,000,15\\,{000}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(46\\,700<X<56\\,150).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(46\\,700<X<56\\,150) & =P(\\frac{46\\,700-50\\,000}{15\\,000}<Z<\\frac{56\\,150-50\\,000}{15\\,000}) \\\\  & =P(-0.22<Z<0.41)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.22<Z<0.41) & =P(Z<0.41)-P(Z\\le -0.22) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.41)-P(Z\\le -0.22) \\\\  & =Φ(0.41)-Φ(-0.22)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.41) & =0.6591 \\\\ Φ(-0.22) & =0.4129.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.22<Z<0.41) & =Φ(0.41)-Φ(-0.22) \\\\  & =0.6591-0.4129 \\\\  & =0.2462 \\\\  & ≈0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected employee's income is more than $$46\\,700$ but less than $$56\\,150$ is approximately $0.25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68479ded-5f29-5fd9-9604-e25bef7d5e32" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability When a Population Parameter is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d8cfe9-4b93-5f75-80a5-535b163dfe29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516214} {:answer/id #uuid "34d6d3c7-1689-5248-895a-b81d7cf970d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516223} {:answer/id #uuid "6466cbf3-e0e7-5e28-a7cc-3d9c12cae870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516331} {:answer/id #uuid "66956326-a3a4-5914-80b0-99ab80e8cc5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516334} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cebdaf-ad45-5124-8538-17b343686fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3cebdaf-ad45-5124-8538-17b343686fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516484} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7a92c6e-2892-5988-ae79-425c23feccca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7012a871-ceb2-5bf5-9d51-cddf37c78344" :question/math-academy-id "q-133054" :question/problem "The average volume of soda in cans is $12$ ounces. Additionally, in $3%$ of cans, the soda volume is less than $11.7$ ounces. Assuming the volume of soda is normally distributed, find the percentage of cans containing less than $12.2$ ounces of soda, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the volume of soda in a randomly selected can. Then we have $X∼N(12,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<12.2).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nWe know that $P(X<11.7)=0.03.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<11.7) & =0.03 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{11.7-12}{\\sigma }) & =0.03 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{-0.3}{\\sigma }) & =0.03 \\\\ \\frac{-0.3}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.03)≈-1.8808.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-0.3}{\\sigma } & =-1.8808 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =6.26933 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{6.26933} \\\\  & ≈0.1595.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<12.2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<12.2) & =P(X\\le 12.2) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{12.2-12}{0.1595}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le 1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25) \\\\  & ≈89.4%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $89.4%$  of soda cans contain less than $12.2$ ounces of soda."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0a05661-7722-5963-b806-be7baa982a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0a05661-7722-5963-b806-be7baa982a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301079} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fef1f38-f1c6-5dcb-ab28-87a9ef1d2f28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c76de2b6-dcfd-5626-8f87-83f3745840b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-324458" :question/problem "In electronics manufacturing, the average resistance of a certain type of resistor is $100\\,Ω.$ However, $5%$ of resistors have resistance no more than $96.5\\,Ω.$ Assuming that resistor resistances are normally distributed, find the standard deviation, $\\sigma ,$ of the resistance, **rounded to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.25$ | $0.20$ | $0.15$ | $0.10$ | $0.05$ | $0.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,{Φ}^{-1}(p)\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$Ω$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the resistance (in ohms). Then we have $X∼N(100,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 96.5)=0.05.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 96.5) & =0.05 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{96.5-100}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ -\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.05).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.05)=-1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & =-1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & =1.6449 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3.5}{1.6449} \\\\  & ≈2.13\\,Ω.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "264f5867-7c47-5f07-9b9c-b3815c6cd840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53.10\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493452} {:answer/id #uuid "73143a86-4466-59b0-9d13-697c03f90f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40.50\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493562} {:answer/id #uuid "975eb98b-4ab0-5014-8bea-9fcf9cfaaba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50.50\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493630} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6926a3-0298-524c-9ffa-d17531745d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52.15\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493768} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5d72e2-1423-5d98-a00f-6cea45e898da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48.05\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "975eb98b-4ab0-5014-8bea-9fcf9cfaaba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50.50\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493630} :answer-field/id #uuid "7782954d-0945-5a09-a4ad-d4eaf2acb6cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d916a01a-c116-520b-be92-bf93eddd6997" :question/math-academy-id "q-95126" :question/problem "The lengths of babies at birth in a given population are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{cm}$ and a standard deviation of $6\\,\\text{cm}.$ Additionally, the length of $60%$ of the newborns falls between $0.9\\mu \\,\\text{cm}$ and $1.1\\mu \\,\\text{cm}.$ Find the mean $\\mu $ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the length of a randomly selected newborn. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{6}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.6.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.6 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.6 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.3 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.3 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.8 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6}) & =0.8 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6}) & =0.8 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{6} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.8)≈0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =5.0496 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{5.0496}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈50.50\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
